        "msg": [
          {
            "type": "HTML",
            "data": "\u003cdiv class\u003d\"markdown-body\"\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eBASH vs HIVE\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eTASK: Count the rows in the dataset.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHIVE: \u003ccode\u003e37 seconds\u003c/code\u003e\u003cbr /\u003e\nBASH: \u003ccode\u003e17 seconds\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe delta is of a whopping \u003ccode\u003e20 seconds\u003c/code\u003e.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThis is primarily due to their architectural design.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eBash operates directly on the filesystem with minimal overhead, making it quick for small and local tasks.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHive, on the other hand goes through multiple layers to process the data. It involves parsing SQL, accessing metadata from metastore, applying schema checks and later triggering a MR | Tez job.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhile Hive was slow for this task, it is optimal for a larger and complex data job.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003c/div\u003e"
          }
